Relevant columns and how they are used  

• `starttime` (TIMESTAMP): departure time ➜ create 2‑minute buckets.  
• `stoptime` (TIMESTAMP): return time (not strictly needed for the metric).  
• `start_station_id` (INT64): station where riders leave ➜ must equal `end_station_id` for a “loop” ride and identifies the bucket.  
• `end_station_id` (INT64): station where riders arrive ➜ denominator = all trips ending here.  
• `start_station_name`, `end_station_name` (STRING): human‑readable station label (added for the report).  
• `tripduration` (INT64) and lat/lon fields: not used for the requested metric, but useful for exploring the data.  

Main conditions that are likely to appear  

• `start_station_id = end_station_id`  (only trips that start & end at the same dock)  
• multiple trips sharing the same 2‑minute slot:  
  `FLOOR(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime)/120)` or `TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120))`  

Below are ten exploratory → analytical BigQuery SQL snippets (all use a 20‑row LIMIT) that steadily build toward the final answer.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at raw Citibike trips
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Trips that start and end at the same station (loop rides)
SELECT start_station_id, end_station_id, starttime, stoptime
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
WHERE start_station_id = end_station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Distinct list of loop‑ride stations
SELECT DISTINCT start_station_id
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
WHERE start_station_id = end_station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Total number of trips ending at each station (denominator preview)
SELECT end_station_id, COUNT(*) AS total_trips_ending
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
GROUP BY end_station_id
ORDER BY total_trips_ending DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Create 2‑minute buckets for one sample station and see how many trips fall in each
SELECT
  start_station_id,
  TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120)) AS bucket_start,
  COUNT(*) AS trips_in_bucket
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
WHERE start_station_id = end_station_id            -- loop rides only
  AND start_station_id = 72                        -- sample station
GROUP BY start_station_id, bucket_start
ORDER BY bucket_start
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Flag each loop trip as “group” or “solo” (sample 20 rows)
SELECT
  start_station_id,
  starttime,
  bucket_start,
  trips_in_bucket,
  trips_in_bucket > 1 AS is_group_ride
FROM (
  SELECT
    *,
    TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120)) AS bucket_start,
    COUNT(*) OVER(
      PARTITION BY start_station_id,
                   TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120))
    ) AS trips_in_bucket
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
  WHERE start_station_id = end_station_id
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Aggregate the number of group‑ride TRIPS for each station
WITH marked AS (
  SELECT
    start_station_id,
    TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120)) AS bucket_start,
    COUNT(*) OVER(
      PARTITION BY start_station_id,
                   TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120))
    ) AS trips_in_bucket
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
  WHERE start_station_id = end_station_id
)
SELECT
  start_station_id,
  COUNTIF(trips_in_bucket > 1) AS group_ride_trips
FROM marked
GROUP BY start_station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Combine numerator (group trips) and denominator (all ending trips)
WITH group_ct AS (
  SELECT
    start_station_id AS station,
    COUNTIF(trips_in_bucket > 1) AS group_trips
  FROM (
    SELECT
      start_station_id,
      TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120)) AS bucket_start,
      COUNT(*) OVER(
        PARTITION BY start_station_id,
                     TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120))
      ) AS trips_in_bucket
    FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
    WHERE start_station_id = end_station_id
  )
  GROUP BY station
),
total_ct AS (
  SELECT end_station_id AS station, COUNT(*) AS total_trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
  GROUP BY station
)
SELECT
  g.station,
  g.group_trips,
  t.total_trips,
  SAFE_DIVIDE(g.group_trips, t.total_trips) AS proportion_group
FROM group_ct g
JOIN total_ct t USING (station)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Attach station names for readability
WITH group_ct AS (
  SELECT
    start_station_id AS station,
    COUNTIF(trips_in_bucket > 1) AS group_trips
  FROM (
    SELECT
      start_station_id,
      TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120)) AS bucket_start,
      COUNT(*) OVER(
        PARTITION BY start_station_id,
                     TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120))
      ) AS trips_in_bucket
    FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
    WHERE start_station_id = end_station_id
  )
  GROUP BY station
),
total_ct AS (
  SELECT end_station_id AS station, COUNT(*) AS total_trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
  GROUP BY station
)
SELECT
  s.name AS station_name,
  g.group_trips,
  t.total_trips,
  SAFE_DIVIDE(g.group_trips, t.total_trips) AS proportion_group
FROM group_ct g
JOIN total_ct t USING (station)
LEFT JOIN `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_stations` s
ON CAST(s.station_id AS INT64) = g.station
ORDER BY proportion_group DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: top 10 stations by highest proportion of group rides
WITH loop_trips AS (
  SELECT
    trip_id,                             -- use ROW_NUMBER surrogate if no id
    start_station_id,
    end_station_id,
    starttime
  FROM (
    SELECT
      ROW_NUMBER() OVER() AS trip_id,    -- surrogate id
      *
    FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
  )
  WHERE start_station_id = end_station_id
),
group_flag AS (
  SELECT
    start_station_id,
    trip_id,
    COUNT(*) OVER(
      PARTITION BY start_station_id,
                   TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(120*DIV(UNIX_SECONDS(starttime),120))
    ) AS trips_in_bucket
  FROM loop_trips
),
group_ct AS (
  SELECT
    start_station_id AS station,
    COUNTIF(trips_in_bucket > 1) AS group_trips
  FROM group_flag
  GROUP BY station
),
total_ct AS (
  SELECT end_station_id AS station, COUNT(*) AS total_trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
  GROUP BY station
)
SELECT
  s.name AS station_name,
  g.group_trips,
  t.total_trips,
  SAFE_DIVIDE(g.group_trips, t.total_trips) AS proportion_group
FROM group_ct g
JOIN total_ct t USING (station)
LEFT JOIN `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_stations` s
ON CAST(s.station_id AS INT64) = g.station
ORDER BY proportion_group DESC
LIMIT 20;      -- shows the top 10 (plus extra rows for tie‑handling) within 20
```

